to retain the contents of the IV, you should\0A *         either save it manually or use the cipher module instead.\0A *\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ctx      The AES context to use for encryption or decryption.\0A *                 It must be initialized and bound to a key.\0A * \5Cparam mode     The AES operation: #MBEDTLS_AES_ENCRYPT or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_AES_DECRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam length   The length of the input data in Bytes. This must be a\0A *                 multiple of the block size (\5Cc 16 Bytes).\0A * \5Cparam iv       Initialization vector (updated after use).\0A *                 It must be a readable and writeable buffer of \5Cc 16 Bytes.\0A * \5Cparam input    The buffer holding the input data.\0A *                 It must be readable and of size \5Cp length Bytes.\0A * \5Cparam output   The buffer holding the output data.\0A *                 It must be writeable and of size \5Cp length Bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 on success.\0A * \5Creturn         #MBEDTLS_ERR_AES_INVALID_INPUT_LENGTH\0A *                 on failure.\0A */\0AMBEDTLS_CHECK_RETURN_TYPICAL\0Aint mbedtls_aes_crypt_cbc(mbedtls_aes_context *ctx,\0A                          int mode,\0A                          size_t length,\0A                          unsigned char iv[16],\0A                          const unsigned char *input,\0A                          unsigned char *output);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_CIPHER_MODE_CBC */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_CIPHER_MODE_XTS)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief      This function performs an AES-XTS encryption or decryption\0A *             operation for an entire XTS data unit.\0A *\0A *             AES-XTS encrypts or decrypts blocks based on their location as\0A *             defined by a data unit number. The data unit number must be\0A *             provided by \5Cp data_unit.\0A *\0A *             NIST SP 800-38E limits the maximum size of a data unit to 2^20\0A *             AES blocks. If the data unit is larger than this, this function\0A *             returns #MBEDTLS_ERR_AES_INVALID_INPUT_LENGTH.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ctx          The AES XTS context to use for AES XTS operations.\0A *                     It must be initialized and bound to a key.\0A * \5Cparam mode         The AES operation: #MBEDTLS_AES_ENCRYPT or\0A *                     #MBEDTLS_AES_DECRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam length       The length of a data unit in Bytes. This can be any\0A *                     length between 16 bytes and 2^24 bytes inclusive\0A *                     (between 1 and 2^20 block cipher blocks).\0A * \5Cparam data_unit    The address of the data unit encoded as an array of 16\0A *                     bytes in little-endian format. For disk encryption, this\0A *                     is typically the index of the block device sector that\0A *                     contains the data.\0A * \5Cparam input        The buffer holding the input data (which is an entire\0A *                     data unit). This function reads \5Cp length Bytes from \5Cp\0A *                     input.\0A * \5Cparam output       The buffer holding the output data (which is an entire\0A *                     data unit). This function writes \5Cp length Bytes to \5Cp\0A *                     output.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn             \5Cc 0 on success.\0A * \5Creturn             #MBEDTLS_ERR_AES_INVALID_INPUT_LENGTH if \5Cp length is\0A *                     smaller than an AES block in size (16 Bytes) or if \5Cp\0A *                     length is larger than 2^20 blocks (16 MiB).\0A */\0AMBEDTLS_CHECK_RETURN_TYPICAL\0Aint mbedtls_aes_crypt_xts(mbedtls_aes_xts_context *ctx,\0A                          int mode,\0A                          size_t length,\0A                          const unsigned char data_unit[16],\0A                          const unsigned char *input,\0A                          unsigned char *output);\0A#endif /* MBEDTLS_CIPHER_MODE_XTS */\0A\0A#if defined(MBEDTLS_CIPHER_MODE_CFB)\0A/**\0A * \5Cbrief This function performs an AES-CFB128 encryption or decryption\0A *        operation.\0A *\0A *        It performs the operation defined in the \5Cp mode\0A *        parameter (encrypt or decrypt), on the input data buffer\0A *        defined in the \5Cp input parameter.\0A *\0A *        For CFB, you must set up the context with mbedtls_aes_setkey_enc(),\0A *        regardless of whether you are performing an encryption or decryption\0A *        operation, that is, regardless of the \5Cp mode parameter. This is\0A *        because CFB mode uses the same key schedule for encryption and\0A *        decryption.\0A *\0A * \5Cnote  Upon exit, the content of the IV is updated so that you can\0A *        call the same function again on the next\0A *        block(s) of data and get the same result as if it was\0A *        encrypted in one call. This allows a \22streaming\22 usage.\0A *        If you need to retain the contents of the\0A *        IV, you must either save it manually or use the cipher\0A *        module instead.\0A *\0A *\0A * \5Cparam ctx      The AES context to use for encryption or decryption.\0A *                 It must be initialized and bound to a key.\0A * \5Cparam mode     The AES operation: #MBEDTLS_AES_ENCRYPT or\0A *                 #MBEDTLS_AES_DECRYPT.\0A * \5Cparam length   The length of the input data in Bytes.\0A * \5Cparam iv_off   The offset in IV (updated after use).\0A *                 It must point to a valid \5Cc size_t.\0A * \5Cparam iv       The initialization vector (updated after use).\0A *                 It must be a readable and writeable buffer of \5Cc 16 Bytes.\0A * \5Cparam input    The buffer holding the input data.\0A *                 It must be readable and of size \5Cp length Bytes.\0A * \5Cparam output   The buffer holding the output data.\0A *                 It must be writeable and of size \5Cp length Bytes.\0A *\0A * \5Creturn         \5Cc 0 on success.\0A */\0AMBEDTLS_CHECK_RETURN_TYPICAL\0Aint mbedtls_aes_crypt_cfb128(mbedtls_aes_context *ctx,\0A       